> I have a raster image in a adobe illustrator image, and I want to change the colors inside of it. How can I do that?
**assistant**:
> To change the colors of a raster image within Adobe Illustrator, you can use the "Edit Colors" feature and image tracing. Here's a step-by-step guide:
> 
> 1. **Select the Raster Image:**
>    - Open your Illustrator file and select the raster image you want to change. You can click on it directly with the Selection Tool (V).
> 
> 2. **Image Trace (Optional):**
>    - If you want to treat different areas of your raster image as separate vector elements, consider using Image Trace. Go to `Object > Image Trace > Make` or find the "Image Trace" button in the Properties panel.
>    - Adjust the settings in the Image Trace panel to suit your needs. For high fidelity, use "High Fidelity Photo" or "Low Fidelity Photo" depending on the desired quality and detail.
>    - Once satisfied, click "Expand" in the top toolbar to convert the image into editable vector paths.
> 
> 3. **Edit Colors (For vector):**
>    - If you’ve converted the raster image to vector paths, you can now edit colors. Select the objects or paths you want to change, and then go to `Edit > Edit Colors > Recolor Artwork`.
>    - In the Recolor Artwork dialog, you can change each color individually, set global color changes, or use presets.
>    - Once done, click OK to apply the changes.
> 
> Alternatively, if you want to alter the colors directly on the raster image without vectorizing:
> 
> 4. **Adjust with Effects:**
>    - Select the raster image, and go to `Edit > Edit Colors > Adjust Color Balance`.
>    - In the Adjust Color Balance dialog, you can modify the sliders to change the overall color tones of the image.
>    - Apply changes once you are satisfied.
> 
> 5. **Use Transparency and Overlay:**
>    - Sometimes adding a colored shape with a blend mode over your image can give you the desired color adjustment.
>    - Create a new shape over the raster image, set its color, and change the blending mode (e.g. Multiply, Overlay) using the Transparency panel.
> 
> This approach provides flexibility depending on how much control you want over the color changes. Let me know if you need any further assistance or detailed guidance on a particular step!
