> Where do I adjust the settings for Image Trace?
**assistant**:
> To adjust the settings for Image Trace in Adobe Illustrator, follow these steps:
> 
> 1. **Select the Raster Image:**
>    - Click on the raster image with the Selection Tool (V) to select it.
> 
> 2. **Open the Image Trace Panel:**
>    - There are a couple of ways to access the Image Trace options:
>      - Go to the top menu and select `Window > Image Trace` to open the Image Trace panel.
>      - Alternatively, with your raster image selected, you can click the "Image Trace" button in the control panel at the top of the workspace.
> 
> 3. **Adjust the Settings:**
>    - In the Image Trace panel, you'll see several preset options (like "High Fidelity Photo," "Low Fidelity Photo," "3 Colors," etc.). You can select one of these presets for a starting point.
>    - Below the preset options, you’ll find customizable settings:
>      - **Threshold:** Adjusts the cutoff for determining whether pixels are black or white.
>      - **Paths:** Controls how closely the traced path follows the original pixel artwork.
>      - **Corners:** Adjusts the sharpness of corners in the traced image.
>      - **Noise:** Specifies how much noise to ignore when tracing. Higher values mean smaller details will be ignored.
>      - **Method:** Choose between "Abutted" or "Overlapping" paths depending on your needs for how shapes interact.
>      - **Preview:** Check this box to preview your changes in real-time as you adjust the settings.
> 
> 4. **Apply the Trace:**
>    - Once you’ve configured the settings to your liking, click the "Trace" button at the bottom of the Image Trace panel. 
>    - After tracing, click "Expand" in the control panel or go to `Object > Image Trace > Expand` to convert the traced image into editable vector paths.
> 
> 5. **Fine-Tune as Needed:**
>    - You can always return to the Image Trace panel for further adjustments if you're not satisfied with the initial trace.
> 
> Let me know if you need help with any specific settings or further clarification!
